.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt LOCKUP 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm lockup
.Nd the soft-lockup, hard-lockup and hung-task detectors
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The kernel watches for three hangs, as Linux's lockup watchdogs and
hung-task detector do, and logs a report of each:
.Bl -tag -width "hard lockup"
.It soft lockup
A processor that has not scheduled for 2 x
.Va watchdog_thresh
seconds, 20 by default, a loop in the kernel with interrupts on:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
watchdog: BUG: soft lockup - CPU#0 stuck for 22s! [name:pid]
.Ed
.It hard lockup
A processor whose timer interrupts have stopped for
.Va watchdog_thresh
seconds, a loop with interrupts off:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
watchdog: watchdog detected hard LOCKUP on cpu 1
.Ed
.Pp
A processor with a cycles counter, Intel's architectural performance
monitor or AMD's core counters, gives up a counter to raise an NMI
each
.Va watchdog_thresh
seconds, which checks the processor itself; the boot logs
.Ql nmi_watchdog: enabled: permanently consumes one hw-PMU counter .
Without one, each processor checks the next, the buddy check, and the
boot logs
.Ql watchdog: hard lockup: buddy check of the next processor .
A processor that waits for the kernel's big lock is not reported for its
wait; the holder is.
.It hung task
A thread in uninterruptible sleep, the state D of
.Xr ps 1 ,
other than one SIGKILL would end, as a
.Xr vfork 2
parent's wait for its child,
whose switch count has not moved for
.Va hung_task_timeout_secs
seconds, 120 by default:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
hung_task: INFO: task name:pid blocked for more than 120 seconds.
.Ed
.El
.Pp
Each report gives the processor and thread, the registers and the
return addresses of the stack.
The kernel has no symbol table;
.Ic addr2line -f -e kernel.debug ADDRESS
on the build host names an address.
.Sh FILES
Each setting is a file of
.Pa /proc/sys/kernel
and a boot option of the same name, or
.Ql sysctl.kernel.NAME= ;
a write that changes
.Pa watchdog ,
.Pa soft_watchdog ,
.Pa nmi_watchdog
or
.Pa watchdog_thresh
restarts the detectors at each processor's next tick, and another takes
effect within a sample period, 2 x
.Va watchdog_thresh
/ 5 seconds.
.Bl -tag -width "hung_task_check_interval_secs"
.It Pa watchdog
1 runs the soft and hard detectors, 0 stops both; it reads 1 while either
is on.
.It Pa soft_watchdog
1 runs the soft-lockup detector.
.It Pa nmi_watchdog
1 runs the hard-lockup detector.
Where it cannot run, with no counter and one processor, the file is mode
0444, an open for writing fails with
.Er EACCES ,
and it reads 0.
.It Pa watchdog_thresh
The hard-lockup threshold in seconds, 0 to 60, 10 by default; 0 stops
both detectors.
.It Pa softlockup_panic , Pa hardlockup_panic , Pa hung_task_panic
1 ends a report of that kind in a panic:
.Ql softlockup: hung tasks ,
.Ql Hard LOCKUP
or
.Ql hung_task: blocked tasks .
.It Pa softlockup_all_cpu_backtrace , Pa hardlockup_all_cpu_backtrace , Pa hung_task_all_cpu_backtrace
1 adds every other processor's registers and stack to a report of that
kind, from an NMI; a processor halted in its idle wait is named and
skipped.
.It Pa hung_task_timeout_secs
Seconds in D before a thread is reported, 120 by default; 0 stops the
detector.
.It Pa hung_task_check_interval_secs
Seconds between checks, 0 for the timeout.
.It Pa hung_task_check_count
The most threads a check reads, 32768 on i686 and 4194304 on x86-64 by
default.
.It Pa hung_task_warnings
The reports left, 10 by default; each counts it down, and -1 never stops
them.
.El
.Pp
The boot options
.Cm nmi_watchdog=panic ,
.Cm nopanic ,
.Cm 0
or
.Cm 1 ,
.Cm softlockup_panic= ,
.Cm nowatchdog ,
.Cm nosoftlockup
and
.Cm watchdog_thresh=
read as Linux's do.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The kernel's debug shell holds a lockup of each kind for a test:
.Ic lockup soft 30 ,
.Ic lockup hard 30
or
.Ic lockup hung 300 .
Root does the same from a shell through
.Xr debugfs 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
echo 2 > /proc/sys/kernel/watchdog_thresh
echo hard 15 > /sys/kernel/debug/test_lockup
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr watchdog 4 ,
.Xr debugfs 5 ,
.Xr proc 5 ,
.Xr rach2 7
.Sh STANDARDS
The messages, settings and defaults are Linux 6.12's, with the first
letter of a message in lower case where Linux has a capital.
